  </div>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Spend small, early</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Free labs and write-ups first.</li>
        <li>One practical badge such as PNPT, at a fraction of the CEH training fee.</li>
        <li>Ask your employer to fund OSCP once you are on the job.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Spend big, late or never</div>
      <ul>
        <li>A multi-thousand-dollar course before you have finished one free lab.</li>
        <li>OSCP as your first purchase with no base in Linux and networks.</li>
        <li>A stack of badges with no report to show.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Keep total learning spend inside a plan. A strict starting rule is about 10% of the family education budget for the
    college fee, with the rest for a laptop, labs, books and later badges. Going well above 20% of the whole budget needs
    clear proof of value, and loans for a hacking course are a poor bet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="labs">Free labs and the proof ladder</h2>

