  <h2 id="business">Who pays, and how they trust you</h2>

  <p>
    In a salaried pentest job your employer pays and an interview is the door, so this layer matters mainly for your
    next move and for the side route. For your own firm or freelance tests, the payer is a company that needs an
    independent test, often because a customer, an auditor or a regulator asked for one.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Positioning:</strong> say who you test and what. 'Web application tests for early-stage software companies' beats 'ethical hacker'.</li>
    <li><strong>Proof:</strong> a redacted sample report, your public write-ups and one referral who will vouch for your care with live systems.</li>
    <li><strong>First skill to learn:</strong> a clearly scoped, fixed-price offer with a written scope and an agreed start and end. Scope is both your sales page and your legal cover.</li>
    <li><strong>One number to watch:</strong> how many scoping conversations turn into signed engagements.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Referrals from developers, CTOs and founders you already know convert better than cold messages. Build clear English
    writing and a small network early. For bug bounty there is no client at all, so the sales layer there is your
    reputation on the platform and the quality of your reports. A fuller view of selling expertise is in
    <a href="/blog/freelancing-business/how-to-become-an-independent-consultant-india/">how to become an independent consultant</a>.
  </p>

